# Skill Trees — environment settings.
#
# Copy to .env (which git ignores) and run the server with Node's built-in
# loader — no package needed:
#
#   cd backend
#   node --env-file=../.env server.js
#
# Everything here is optional. With nothing set, the site runs exactly as
# before: username-and-password accounts only, with no passkeys and no
# "Continue with ..." buttons. The startup log says whether passkeys and
# each provider are on and, for any half-configured, what is missing. Secrets are never logged and never
# sent to the browser — keep this file out of version control.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the site

# PORT=3001

# The origin people reach the site at: scheme, host and port only, no path.
# Every OAuth redirect URI is built from this and never from the request's
# Host header, which whoever sends the request controls. Required for any
# provider below to be switched on. Must be https, except for localhost.
#
# It also switches on passkeys. Their RP ID is this origin's host, and a
# passkey only ever works on the RP ID it was made for — so this should be
# the one canonical host, and changing the host later strands every passkey
# made before (they stay listed and removable, but can't sign in). A host
# given as an IP address leaves passkeys off: WebAuthn needs a domain name
# (localhost counts). For local development: http://localhost:3001.
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://skilltrees.example

# --------------------------------------------------- sign in with GitHub
#
# An OAuth App (github.com -> Settings -> Developer settings -> OAuth Apps).
# Authorization callback URL: <PUBLIC_ORIGIN>/api/auth/oauth/github/callback
# No scopes are requested: GitHub then grants read access to the public
# profile and nothing else. Accounts are keyed on GitHub's numeric user id,
# not the login, which can be renamed.
# GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --------------------------------------------------- sign in with Google
#
# An OAuth client ID of type "Web application" (Google Cloud console ->
# APIs & Services -> Credentials). Authorized redirect URI:
#   <PUBLIC_ORIGIN>/api/auth/oauth/google/callback
# Scopes requested: openid profile email (the address only suggests a
# username for a new account; it is never used to find or link one).
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ----------------------------------- sign in with any OpenID Connect provider
#
# Your organisation's SSO (Keycloak, Okta, Entra ID, Authentik, ...). Found
# through <OIDC_ISSUER>/.well-known/openid-configuration, whose "issuer" must
# match OIDC_ISSUER exactly — trailing slash included. The provider has to
# support the authorization code flow with PKCE (S256) and a client secret.
# Redirect URI to register: <PUBLIC_ORIGIN>/api/auth/oauth/oidc/callback
# OIDC_NAME is the button label: "Continue with <OIDC_NAME>".
# OIDC_ISSUER=https://sso.example.com/realms/staff
# OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
# OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OIDC_NAME=Company SSO
